1
00:00:00,033 --> 00:00:08,433
This is moving water. Every arrow shows which way the water goes at that spot, and how fast.

2
00:00:08,433 --> 00:00:15,033
For almost two hundred years, one set of equations has described flows like this.

3
00:00:15,033 --> 00:00:20,733
And for almost a century, mathematicians have asked one question about them:

4
00:00:20,733 --> 00:00:28,567
can a smooth flow tear itself apart, with its speed shooting to infinity at a single point?

5
00:00:28,567 --> 00:00:39,467
In September 2026, OpenAI released a paper claiming the answer is yes, if you are allowed to push the fluid in just the right way.

6
00:00:39,467 --> 00:00:46,500
Let's see what that means, how it works, and how sure we can be.

7
00:00:52,766 --> 00:00:59,300
At every point and every moment, water has a velocity: a speed and a direction.

8
00:00:59,300 --> 00:01:09,633
The Navier–Stokes equations tell you how all those arrows change over time. They balance four effects.

9
00:01:09,633 --> 00:01:16,566
First, the fluid carries itself along. Fast water sweeps its own speed downstream.

10
00:01:16,566 --> 00:01:25,900
This is what makes eddies and swirls, and it is the only effect that can make the flow more extreme.

11
00:01:25,900 --> 00:01:34,133
Second, friction smooths things out. Neighbouring bits of fluid drag on each other and even out their differences.

12
00:01:34,133 --> 00:01:43,333
How strong that is depends on a number called the viscosity: high for honey, low for water.

13
00:01:43,333 --> 00:01:47,000
Third, pressure keeps the volume fixed.

14
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:53,533
Water cannot be squeezed, so pressure pushes back wherever the flow would compress it.

15
00:01:53,533 --> 00:02:00,533
Squash a blob one way, and it must stretch the other way.

16
00:02:00,533 --> 00:02:11,033
And fourth, there may be an outside force: a stirring spoon, or gravity, pushing on the fluid.

17
00:02:11,033 --> 00:02:18,633
Written in symbols, these four effects make the Navier–Stokes equations. You do not need to read them.

18
00:02:18,633 --> 00:02:28,566
What matters is the tug of war: the fluid carrying itself along can sharpen the flow, and friction smooths it.

19
00:02:28,566 --> 00:02:37,333
So: a velocity at every point, and four effects that change it.

20
00:02:41,566 --> 00:02:49,266
Start with a perfectly smooth flow: no jumps, no kinks, a finite speed everywhere.

21
00:02:49,266 --> 00:02:53,566
The equations tell you how it evolves.

22
00:02:53,566 --> 00:02:55,933
Does it stay smooth forever?

23
00:02:55,933 --> 00:03:03,133
Or can the self-carrying effect beat friction, squeezing the motion into a smaller and smaller region,

24
00:03:03,133 --> 00:03:09,100
until at some moment the speed at a point becomes infinite?

25
00:03:09,100 --> 00:03:18,533
That event is called a blowup. If it can happen, the equations stop predicting what comes next.

26
00:03:18,533 --> 00:03:23,333
People have worked on this since Jean Leray in 1934.

27
00:03:23,333 --> 00:03:28,266
We know breakdowns would have to be confined to a tiny set of points.

28
00:03:28,266 --> 00:03:34,833
We know blowups happen in modified versions of the equations, and in related equations,

29
00:03:34,833 --> 00:03:40,333
built by amplifying disturbances on smaller and smaller scales.

30
00:03:40,333 --> 00:03:48,300
But for the real three-dimensional equations, the question stayed open.

31
00:03:48,300 --> 00:03:57,866
In 2000 it became one of the seven Millennium Prize Problems, with a million dollars for a solution.

32
00:03:57,866 --> 00:04:01,533
The official problem offers four ways to win.

33
00:04:01,533 --> 00:04:08,133
Two say the flow stays smooth forever, two say it can break down, in two settings:

34
00:04:08,133 --> 00:04:16,066
all of space, or a box whose opposite walls are glued together, so the fluid wraps around.

35
00:04:16,066 --> 00:04:24,100
The smooth options must hold for every smooth starting flow, with no outside force at all.

36
00:04:24,100 --> 00:04:32,933
The breakdown options need just one example, and they allow a smooth outside force, as long as it fades away.

37
00:04:32,933 --> 00:04:38,433
The paper claims these two, C and D.

38
00:04:38,433 --> 00:04:49,000
So the question is whether a smooth flow can blow up, and the paper claims an example that does.

39
00:04:53,233 --> 00:05:00,500
Allowing a force sounds like cheating. Here is the cheat. Draw any flow you like that blows up.

40
00:05:00,500 --> 00:05:10,067
Plug it into the equations, and call whatever is left over "the force". The equations now hold, by definition.

41
00:05:10,067 --> 00:05:19,400
But the rules forbid this. The force itself must be perfectly smooth, at every moment, including the moment of blowup.

42
00:05:19,400 --> 00:05:26,333
And the force you would need to hold up a blowup almost always blows up too.

43
00:05:26,333 --> 00:05:32,367
So the real challenge is to find a breakdown that a smooth, calm push can sustain.

44
00:05:32,367 --> 00:05:39,333
Each term in the equation may explode, but they must cancel so precisely that the leftover,

45
00:05:39,333 --> 00:05:45,500
the force, stays smooth right through the singular moment.

46
00:05:45,500 --> 00:05:55,600
That is the whole difficulty: a smooth push, and a flow that tears itself apart anyway.

47
00:05:59,833 --> 00:06:08,667
Here is what the paper claims, for every value of the viscosity. The fluid starts completely at rest.

48
00:06:08,667 --> 00:06:18,500
A smooth force acts on it, only inside a bounded region of space, and only for a bounded stretch of time.

49
00:06:18,500 --> 00:06:26,367
Up to time one, the flow is perfectly smooth, and its total energy stays bounded.

50
00:06:26,367 --> 00:06:33,733
And as time approaches one, the speed near the centre grows without any limit.

51
00:06:33,733 --> 00:06:42,733
So no smooth solution with bounded energy, driven by the same force from the same start, can continue for all time.

52
00:06:42,733 --> 00:06:53,400
That is breakdown option C, and the same example, placed in a wrap-around box, gives option D.

53
00:06:53,400 --> 00:06:57,667
How can the speed be infinite while the energy is finite?

54
00:06:57,667 --> 00:07:03,433
Because the fast motion happens in a region that shrinks as time runs out.

55
00:07:03,433 --> 00:07:10,467
It shrinks fast enough that the energy inside it actually goes to zero.

56
00:07:10,467 --> 00:07:24,400
So: a fluid at rest, a smooth push, finite energy, and infinite speed at time one. Now, how is it built?

57
00:07:28,600 --> 00:07:33,767
The construction has three regions: a collapsing core in the middle,

58
00:07:33,767 --> 00:07:42,367
a ring-shaped zone around it called the annulus, and a calm exterior outside.

59
00:07:42,367 --> 00:07:51,533
Start with the core. Picture a vertical axis through the centre. Near it, fluid spirals inward.

60
00:07:51,533 --> 00:07:57,933
As it moves inward, it spins faster, like a figure skater pulling in their arms.

61
00:07:57,933 --> 00:08:05,700
A parcel of fluid that keeps its angular momentum must spin faster at a smaller radius.

62
00:08:05,700 --> 00:08:12,500
The incoming fluid cannot pile up near the axis, because water cannot be squeezed.

63
00:08:12,500 --> 00:08:21,800
So it escapes up and down along the axis, and that lets the inflow and the spin-up continue.

64
00:08:21,800 --> 00:08:27,233
As time runs out, the whole core shrinks while keeping its shape.

65
00:08:27,233 --> 00:08:31,333
Its width shrinks like the square root of the time left.

66
00:08:31,333 --> 00:08:39,300
Its height shrinks a little more slowly, so the core becomes an ever more slender spinning column.

67
00:08:39,300 --> 00:08:44,933
And its speeds grow without limit, a little faster than one over the width.

68
00:08:44,933 --> 00:08:53,267
Friction keeps fighting the whole time, carrying spin back outward, but it does not win.

69
00:08:53,267 --> 00:09:04,200
So the core is a slender, spinning column, collapsing toward the centre and spinning ever faster.

70
00:09:08,433 --> 00:09:16,367
Inside the core, the flow balances the equations. Outside, the exterior does too.

71
00:09:16,367 --> 00:09:22,333
But joining the two leaves an imbalance in the annulus between them.

72
00:09:22,333 --> 00:09:28,967
On its own, this background flow would need an outside force in the annulus that blows up.

73
00:09:28,967 --> 00:09:38,967
That is exactly the cheat the rules forbid. So the paper makes the fluid's own motion supply the missing push.

74
00:09:38,967 --> 00:09:45,300
It adds pulses: small, rapidly oscillating ripples inside the annulus.

75
00:09:45,300 --> 00:09:52,067
Each one wraps around a complete ring, and lasts only a short time.

76
00:09:52,067 --> 00:10:00,533
Each pulse is started by an exponentially small push. Then the background flow amplifies it.

77
00:10:00,533 --> 00:10:06,667
Here is how. In a spinning flow whose rotation falls off quickly with distance,

78
00:10:06,667 --> 00:10:13,833
a bit of fluid nudged outward keeps a surplus of spin, and that surplus flings it further out.

79
00:10:13,833 --> 00:10:20,067
The effect feeds itself, and the pulse grows.

80
00:10:20,067 --> 00:10:29,667
But the same shearing flow tilts the ripples and packs them closer and closer, until friction wins, and the pulse dies away.

81
00:10:29,667 --> 00:10:35,500
Each pulse grows, and then decays.

82
00:10:35,500 --> 00:10:44,067
Now the key point. A ripple averages to zero: as much fluid moves in as out. But look at what it carries.

83
00:10:44,067 --> 00:10:55,000
Fluid moving outward carries extra spin, and fluid moving inward carries a deficit. Either way, spin is carried outward.

84
00:10:55,000 --> 00:11:01,200
That steady, averaged flow of momentum acts like an internal force on the background.

85
00:11:01,200 --> 00:11:08,500
The pulses are tuned so that it cancels the imbalance in the annulus.

86
00:11:08,500 --> 00:11:14,367
The imbalance has two parts, one for spin and one for vertical motion.

87
00:11:14,367 --> 00:11:22,100
So the paper uses two families of pulses, pointing in different directions, mixed in positive amounts.

88
00:11:22,100 --> 00:11:30,333
The background is shaped so that the target always lies inside the cone between them.

89
00:11:30,333 --> 00:11:40,100
As time runs out, the pulses are placed on ever smaller scales, keeping pace with the collapsing core.

90
00:11:41,133 --> 00:11:47,233
The pulses fix the leading imbalance, but they create new, smaller errors:

91
00:11:47,233 --> 00:11:55,100
from interacting with each other, with the background, and from being switched on and off.

92
00:11:55,100 --> 00:11:59,800
So the paper corrects them, in an infinite cycle of rounds.

93
00:11:59,800 --> 00:12:05,067
Each round makes the remaining error vanish faster at the singular point.

94
00:12:05,067 --> 00:12:10,500
In the end, the leftover error, and every one of its derivatives,

95
00:12:10,500 --> 00:12:18,800
vanishes faster than any power as you approach the singular point.

96
00:12:18,800 --> 00:12:27,333
Outside, the flow is a simple circular motion that spreads exactly like heat. It needs no force at all.

97
00:12:27,333 --> 00:12:35,300
And the whole construction is cut off smoothly, so everything happens in a bounded region.

98
00:12:35,300 --> 00:12:41,433
The force is then defined as the leftover: whatever the final flow still lacks.

99
00:12:41,433 --> 00:12:46,133
Because that leftover vanishes so completely near the singular moment,

100
00:12:46,133 --> 00:12:55,533
the force passes smoothly through time one, and it switches off by time two.

101
00:12:55,533 --> 00:13:03,900
One more step. The rules ask that no smooth solution with the same force and start exists for all time.

102
00:13:03,900 --> 00:13:12,167
The paper shows that any such solution would have to coincide with the constructed one, all the way up to time one.

103
00:13:12,167 --> 00:13:20,100
Two solutions that start together, with one of them well behaved, can never drift apart.

104
00:13:20,100 --> 00:13:29,000
So any rival inherits the blowup, and cannot stay smooth forever.

105
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:33,367
Everything so far is done for one particular viscosity.

106
00:13:33,367 --> 00:13:44,000
Zooming space by the square root of the viscosity turns it into a solution for any viscosity, with the same blowup time.

107
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:53,533
Let's put it together. A collapsing, spinning core. An imbalance in the ring around it, cancelled by amplified pulses.

108
00:13:53,533 --> 00:13:59,267
Infinitely many rounds of corrections. A force equal to the smooth leftover.

109
00:13:59,267 --> 00:14:05,167
And a uniqueness argument that leaves no escape.

110
00:14:08,400 --> 00:14:10,534
So, is it right?

111
00:14:10,534 --> 00:14:18,500
OpenAI also published a proof written in Lean, a language in which a computer checks every logical step.

112
00:14:18,500 --> 00:14:28,300
It is about four hundred and twenty-nine thousand lines long, with no gaps and no extra assumptions.

113
00:14:28,300 --> 00:14:35,500
Its final theorems are checked against a statement of the prize problem that OpenAI did not write,

114
00:14:35,500 --> 00:14:42,434
adapted from a project called Formal Conjectures. If that check holds, the theorem is true.

115
00:14:42,434 --> 00:14:47,434
We have not re-run it ourselves.

116
00:14:47,434 --> 00:14:55,434
But in October, three mathematicians, Bastounis, Circelli and Hansen, pointed out a problem.

117
00:14:55,434 --> 00:15:01,400
According to them, the Lean proof was produced by translating the paper with AI,

118
00:15:01,400 --> 00:15:06,967
and accepting the translation as soon as it compiled.

119
00:15:06,967 --> 00:15:12,734
They found places where the Lean proof quietly proves different steps from the paper:

120
00:15:12,734 --> 00:15:20,667
a weaker bound in one place, and a different argument for a pressure estimate in the uniqueness step.

121
00:15:20,667 --> 00:15:28,634
They do not claim the paper is wrong. Their point is that a computer proof certifies the theorem, not the paper.

122
00:15:28,634 --> 00:15:35,800
The paper's own arguments still need ordinary human review.

123
00:15:35,800 --> 00:15:42,200
So there are three separate questions. Is the theorem true? That is for the Lean check.

124
00:15:42,200 --> 00:15:49,234
Is the paper's proof correct as written? That is for peer review. And has the prize been won?

125
00:15:49,234 --> 00:15:56,334
That is for the Clay Institute, which says it is evaluating the claim.

126
00:15:56,334 --> 00:16:04,534
A computer-checked theorem, and a written proof that still needs review.

127
00:16:08,766 --> 00:16:13,800
If it is right, it settles the Millennium problem as officially written:

128
00:16:13,800 --> 00:16:21,866
the forced Navier–Stokes equations can break down from smooth, harmless-looking starting data.

129
00:16:21,866 --> 00:16:25,866
But it does not show that flows left to themselves blow up.

130
00:16:25,866 --> 00:16:35,600
Without any force, that question is still open, and many mathematicians see it as the physical heart of the problem.

131
00:16:35,600 --> 00:16:39,366
It also does not mean real water blows up.

132
00:16:39,366 --> 00:16:47,466
The equations are a model, real fluids are made of molecules, and this force is specially engineered.

133
00:16:47,466 --> 00:16:53,633
And it changes nothing about weather forecasting or engineering.

134
00:16:53,633 --> 00:17:03,366
A breakdown, driven by a perfectly smooth push. The unforced question remains.

135
00:17:04,400 --> 00:17:14,200
Let's recap. A fluid at rest. A calm, smooth push. A spinning column that narrows and spins up without limit.

136
00:17:14,200 --> 00:17:19,234
A ring of amplified ripples carrying exactly the momentum it needs.

137
00:17:19,234 --> 00:17:25,734
And a force that stays smooth while the flow tears itself apart.

138
00:17:25,734 --> 00:17:34,667
If the proof holds up, the answer to the prize question, as it was written, is yes: smooth flows can break.

139
00:17:34,667 --> 00:17:42,234
Whether they can break all on their own is still a mystery.
